  ok   so does the host owner's, byte for byte the same program and address
  ok   and ONLY the host owner's line arrives: SCM_CREDENTIALS, not obscurity
  ok   and the uid-1001 one is dropped, unstamped, by the receiving kernel

attack 6 of 6 -- PIXGRAB: bind the pixel hand-up address and be handed
the desktop's display lists.
(the attack the new channel invites, and the one that decides whether it
 is sound.  The rendezvous is an ABSTRACT AF_UNIX name with no file mode,
 derived from public facts, so ANYBODY can bind it -- and a receiver-checks
 construction, which is exactly what the keystroke channel correctly uses,
 would here hand every client's screen to whoever bound it first.  Which
 end holds the secret decides which end checks: THE SENDER checks, with
 SO_PEERCRED on the connection it just made.)
  ok   a uid-1001 attacker CAN take the address -- nothing can stop it
  ok   AND NOT ONE CLIENT HANDS IT A DESCRIPTOR: fds=0, no display list
  ok   and there is nothing to read: no scene came back at all
  ok   and the client SAYS SO, by name, on stderr -- a refusal nobody sees is how a desktop stops painting for no stated reason
  ok   the SEGMENT OWNER (what wsysd is on a real boot) binds the same address
  ok   and IS handed the descriptors: the same program, the same address, and the only difference is who the kernel says is listening
  ok   and reads the victim's committed display list out of the memfd -- which is the desktop working, the same bytes wsysd composites

